[Music]  Northern California police officer says  a routine patrol led to a life-changing  event when a homeless woman asked him to  adopt her baby girl John Blackstone  introduces us to a family going above  and beyond the call of duty you like the  bread with three daughters under 8 years  old Jessi and Ashleigh Witten already  had a full house you had a family yes a  complete family they didn't need to add  anybody to it we're planning on adding  anybody to why I think I think it's when  you see a need you just need to show up  and that's what parenthood in our  experience says anyway as a 10-year  veteran of the Santa Rosa Police  Department Jessi befriended a pregnant  homeless woman with a drug addiction  whom he frequently ran into while on  patrol I was trying to get her there  were times I got her shelter at  different different locations sometimes  she would take me up on and sometimes  she wouldn't then his wife met her on a  ride-along last August  her and I got to talking and she was  very obviously pregnant and so I said oh  you're pregnant and she grabbed my hand  and she put it on her stomach and I had  no idea that a year later out the baby I  was feeling in her womb was would be our  daughter in February they got an  unexpected life-changing call the birth  mother had specifically asked for the  Witten's to adopt her child  they agreed despite concerns about the  baby's health you have to think that a  mother who's drug-addicted having a baby  the baby's gonna have some big problems  right we didn't know what to expect we  we knew that we were gonna take her no  matter what but yeah but we were like is  this gonna be something that she's gonna  be hooked up to two tubes the rest of  her life she did go through some  withdrawals she had some shaking and  they were worried about some seizures  now they say she's hitting all the  normal baby milestones and is expected  to lead a healthy life she's got the  sweetest disposition she's so nice she's  ove